{:edb/db-before-t 163 :edb/db-after-t 164 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533476 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:22:47.932Z" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186054637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399137 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186059820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399117 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186064209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398967 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186064211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398889 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186078410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399056 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398995 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399002 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095730 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398896 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} =-10$." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097306 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1074 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095736 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398902 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - e^{3x} = 0$." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097307 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095760 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398908 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1071 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨e^{t} - 4, \\sin t⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097311 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096150 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399124 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1071 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨3t^{2}, e^{t}⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186097376 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186100876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398939 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399090 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399097 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186110579 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398946 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186110589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398974 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186115226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399063 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186115248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398988 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398981 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399043 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399015 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399022 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399029 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186119825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399036 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119234 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186120525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399009 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1071 "Given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$,\nhas the LU decomposition $A = LU$, then $l_{21} + u_{22} =$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186120525 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}],$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119252 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186120528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399050 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t}, \\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}⟩$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0, 1⟩$." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186120528 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398953 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398960 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186124606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399130 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123740 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186125195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398920 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1071 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = xy^{2} - 2yz + z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x, y, z) = (1,-1, 1)$." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186125195 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186147437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399110 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146582 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186148143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398914 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186148143 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399144 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399151 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186175951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399070 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186175991 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399083 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186176576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175564 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186176576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399077 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186176576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186176576 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186176576 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ for the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$(A - 2I)^{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186176576 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-2I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-2I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398932 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183571 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186184832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398926 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184832 1071 "![](images/10/10912ded2166eb7ae28ee81c2936f008bf14eb288ec95969b822dd138be509d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?" 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186184832 1071 "![](images/10/10912ded2166eb7ae28ee81c2936f008bf14eb288ec95969b822dd138be509d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184832 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-\\pi )=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1.\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183679 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186184850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399104 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184850 1071 "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\cot (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?" 13194139533476 17592186045585 false] [17592186184850 1071 "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186184850 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398890 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1069 "q-49468" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1072 #uuid "2058ea27-d95b-5f37-8b22-5c724bfe6f2c" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398889 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=s+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2/5}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(s+1)}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[{(s+1)}^{2/5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s+1)+0 \\\\  & ={(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398891 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186398890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398892 13194139533476 1