f(x)={x}^{3}-12x+4$ have a local minimum?" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399090 1072 #uuid "f31a2fb0-549c-5b45-aecb-95fd4bb53cca" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399090 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-4)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =3((-3)^{2}-4)=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-4)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3((3)^{2}-4)=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=2.$" 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399092 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399093 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399094 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399095 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399096 13194139533476 17592186045585 true] [17592186399091 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533476 17592186045585 true